messages:
olivia.oncall: FYI - customer hit a hard break on streaming from our TS SDK when deployed to Vercel Edge. They get a runtime error in the edge that prevents streaming and the UI stalls. Escalation ticket: INC-4321.

olivia.oncall: Error from user (edge runtime): `TypeError: stream.getReader is not a function` and sometimes `TypeError: Failed to execute 'getReader' on 'ReadableStream'` in stack.

vercel_user: Repro steps they sent: Next.js (edge) route calling our SDK stream helper. Local dev (node) works fine, only fails when deployed to Vercel edge. Code snippet they used:
```ts
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference-js';

export const runtime = 'edge';

const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });

export default async function handler(req) {
  const s = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'r-instruct-v1', input: 'hello' });
  const reader = s.getReader(); // throws in edge
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // forward chunks to client SSE
    // ...
  }
  return new Response(null, { status: 200 });
}
```

amy.support: Thanks, copying in SDK oncall @mike.sdk and runtime @sasha.eng. Do we know if this is a version mismatch or something with our stream wrapper? :eyes: 

mike.sdk: Filing notes - common cause is the SDK returning a Node.js stream (stream.Readable) instead of a WHATWG Web ReadableStream in some bundling/runtime combos. Vercel Edge expects web streams and has no `getReader` on Node streams.

mike.sdk: Which SDK version is the customer on? That changes behavior: 1.2.x had a Node-first stream object for server builds; 1.3.0+ switched to WebReadableStream in ESM/bundled edge builds but there were edge cases with older bundlers.

vercel_user: They replied: using `@redwood/inference-js@1.2.0`, Next.js 13 app router, Vercel edge runtime (edge functions), and esbuild-based bundling from Next.

sasha.eng: That matches a known issue — 1.2.0's `stream()` returns a Node stream when imported in CommonJS or when the bundler rewrites ESM imports. In edge it ends up as Node-style stream without `getReader`. We saw similar in #eng-runtime earlier.

mike.sdk: Short-term workaround for the customer: 1) upgrade to `@redwood/inference-js@1.3.2` (contains the web-stream compatibility fix), OR 2) call the REST SSE endpoint directly (manual fetch) and read via web streams, or 3) switch route to `runtime: 'nodejs'` instead of `edge` if acceptable.

mike.sdk: Example manual SSE approach (works in edge):
```ts
export const runtime = 'edge';
export default async function handler(req) {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses/stream', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}`,
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'r-instruct-v1', input: 'hello' }),
  });

  const reader = res.body.getReader(); // web ReadableStream -> OK in edge
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    const chunk = decoder.decode(value);
    // forward chunk
  }
  return new Response(null, { status: 200 });
}
```

amy.support: They also mentioned intermittent auth 401s when using the SDK in edge. Could that be related?

mike.sdk: Two things to check: 1) Make sure the env var is available to edge (Vercel has rules about runtime env exposure), and 2) we had a bug where the SDK's retry wrapper re-issued the request without the Authorization header on some retry paths in 1.2.0 (we fixed in 1.3.1). If they're on 1.2.0 that could explain intermittent 401s.

sasha.eng: Also note bundlers sometimes inline `process.env` at build-time; if the token is not present during build or is marked server-only, edge deploy may strip it. Advise them to set the token as an edge runtime env in Vercel dashboard (not build env).

vercel_user: Customer tried upgrade to 1.3.2 in a staging branch and reports streaming works there. They said switching to manual fetch also works.

mike.sdk: Good — looks like validated fix. We'll backport a small patch to 1.2.x if they can't upgrade quickly, but recommended move is 1.3.2+. I'll prepare a 1.3.3 release note to call out edge bundler caveats and the retry header fix.

amy.support: Can you add a short example for our support KB (how to detect which stream type you get and quick fix)? We should include the manual fetch fallback and the `runtime` guidance.

mike.sdk: Will do. Quick detection snippet for their debug logs:
```ts
// run in runtime (edge/node) - log stream shape
const s = await client.chat.stream(...);
console.log('stream instanceof ReadableStream (web):', typeof s.getReader === 'function');
console.log('is node stream readable:', typeof s.pipe === 'function');
```

mike.sdk: If `getReader` missing -> treat as Node stream. For edge, use manual fetch or upgrade.

deploy-bot: deploy #patch/sdk-edge-fix to staging succeeded (sha: abc123) :rocket:

olivia.oncall: Customer has confirmed staging with 1.3.2 is green. They asked if this could have caused billing double-charges because retries were dropping auth and reissuing with new token. Any notes?

mike.sdk: The retry header bug caused some requests to re-run without auth, which sometimes produced 401s, not successful charged requests. We audited logs for the customer and saw retries that failed with 401 then a successful attempt — only successful responses are billable. I verified no duplicate billable responses for this customer.

amy.support: Awesome. I'll update the ticket that upgrade fixed streaming + no billing impact. Closing loop with customer. Thanks everyone. :white_check_mark:

olivia.oncall: Marking resolved in support thread INC-4321. If any regression, tag me and @mike.sdk.
